WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:22.920
Karen Wheeler: I do not believe this will necessarily take too long. Another piece of important work

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:31.020
we have been doing with the Audit and Risk
Committee through the year is looking at the

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:38.149
extent to which we have properly embedded
in our organisation the way that we manage,

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:49.800
control and assure the work of NHS England.
We are now three years in to our existence,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.820
and we agreed during the course of the last
year that we needed to move faster on making

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:02.170
sure that we had embedded appropriate controls
and assurance. One part of that work is the

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:12.400
governance work, and what this represents
is trying to implement a way of better managing,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.880
in a systematic way, all of our governance
work across the organisation. So the manual

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:25.480
is the way of doing that. We have taken the
opportunity of course, as well, to update

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:32.470
standing orders, SFIs and delegations to reflect
changes in our management structure, to do

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:39.430
some tidying up, which needed to be reflected,
and to streamline some aspects of the way

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.490
that we operate so that it makes it easier
for the organisation.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.940
This paper is just bringing that to your attention
and asking for your approval. It has already

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:56.420
been gone through by the Audit and Risk Committee,
so I think you can be assured that this is

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:02.880
acceptable to approve.
Malcolm Grant: David, I assume you endorse

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.460
that last comment?
David Roberts: We do.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.349
Malcolm Grant: Good. Everybody has had a copy
of the revised papers with tracked changes;

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:15.629
I am sure everybody has read it. Actually,
I love this sort of thing. There is some really

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:20.629
interesting stuff in there, but on the assurance
that we have had from Karen and from David,

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:26.439
may I ask the Board please to approve it?
Thank you very much.

00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:31.459
Item nine on the agenda is the revised patient
complaints policy.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.700
Karen Wheeler: This, again, reflects some work that we were doing. It is not a substantive change, but

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:47.269
it is obviously important that it comes to
the Board for approval. I chair a customer

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:53.989
contact and complaints programme board on
behalf of NHS England, and we are constantly

00:02:53.989 --> 00:03:00.579
looking at our performance on patient contact
and dealing with complaints across the organisation,

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:07.359
both centrally and in our regions. As part
of that work, we have identified that some

00:03:07.359 --> 00:03:14.499
of the issues that are getting in the way
of prompt response to complaints are a limited

00:03:14.499 --> 00:03:21.139
number of people who are able to sign off
complaint letters, and we expanded that number

00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:29.599
with our regions to hopefully unlock that
bottleneck and improve our general performance.

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:36.040
Those changes have been reflected in policy.
We have also done some tidying up, and also,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.519
to reflect some quality assurance work we
have doing on the way we manage and improve

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:44.790
complaints and assurance. There is a lot more
work to be done; I am sure we will be coming

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:53.299
back with more changes, but again, I think
you can be assured that these are sensible

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:57.799
improvements to make to the way we manage
our complaints.

00:04:01.809 --> 00:04:14.590
Bruce Keogh: Can I just ask you about quality
of the response? Over the years, I have read

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:20.340
a number of letters from different NHS organisations
which are sent to patients, which, if they

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.430
were angry before they had sent the letter,
they would have been incandescent when they

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:31.580
got the response. A lot of it is just about
language, about saying sorry and simple things

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:39.610
like that. Do we have a process to ensure
that these letters are proportionate in the

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:44.639
language, and that they are easy to read and
easy to understand and not just some bureaucratic

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:48.490
diatribe which arrives on someone’s desk?
I have seen plenty of those.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:55.750
Karen Wheeler: I am sure, Bruce, we can always
do better on this, and I see them myself,

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:02.360
as well. We certainly do have a quality assurance
process and a quality assurance framework.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:13.520
We are doing more work to try to extend some
training and best practice across people who

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.840
deal with the complaints, and we have a proper
quality assurance process over the letters

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.849
that go out, but I have no doubt that we can
improve that.

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:27.080
Bruce Keogh: They need to be kind.
Karen Wheeler: They do; they need to be appropriately

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:34.009
apologetic, and they also need to be clear
and simple to understand. However, it is often

00:05:34.009 --> 00:05:44.139
a balance between trying to unpick a complex
complaint and address every issue in it, in

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:53.770
very simple, plain English, and that can sometimes
look bureaucratic. I know exactly what you

00:05:53.770 --> 00:06:00.639
are thinking of.
Victor Adebowale: Do we get feedback on people’s

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:10.539
experience of complaining, as opposed to just
getting a letter? Do we segment that by population

00:06:10.539 --> 00:06:17.620
in any way? Do we know who complains the most?
Karen Wheeler: We are now at a point where

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:24.610
we can start to mine our information about
complaints, because last year we implemented

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:31.120
a contact system so that every complaint and
every contact is recorded systematically.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:38.270
We now have good information that we consult
to mine, so we are starting to do that and

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:42.240
to work out what we are learning from our
complaints.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.669
We also do surveys. We try to get feedback
from people who have complained, so that is

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:53.319
actually quite hard to get that feedback,
but we are constantly thinking about how we

00:06:53.319 --> 00:07:02.879
can do that. I am very happy to bring back
information to you and the Board about what

00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:09.509
we learn from that, as we do it. This is starting
to be available to us now to really investigate.

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:14.360
Richard Barker: My question is essentially
the same one: increasingly, especially as

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:19.789
we use mobile phones and so on, people can
be asked just by text, ‘How do you feel

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:23.960
about the response?’, and I think it would
be instructive to actually address the issue

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.449
that Bruce is raising.
Karen Wheeler: That is exactly the sort of

00:07:27.449 --> 00:07:32.080
thing that we are anxious to try and put in
place to try and get TripAdvisor‑type feedback,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:40.620
as it were: ‘Was this okay for you?’ Clearly,
the one piece of evidence we have which you

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.780
will not be surprised at is the experience
that people that talk about is most likely

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:51.879
related to the outcome, so if they got a good
outcome: so if they are more likely to be

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:56.699
happy; if they got an outcome which they are
not happy with, however well handled it was,

00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:02.610
they are not likely to be happy with that.
Apart from that, we are doing our best to

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.810
try and improve that.
Malcolm Grant: It cannot be a good outcome

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:10.460
if you wait six months to get it. I have been
very taken, talking to people in our committee

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:15.259
teams, at how ready they are to pick up the
phone straight away to discuss it informally

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:22.479
with the complainant, and actually often head
off what might be a growing sense of injustice

00:08:22.479 --> 00:08:26.749
by personal, informal responses at an early
stage.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:30.210
Karen Wheeler: I think you are absolutely
right, Malcolm. That is a really important

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:36.320
point we have been trying to get to, which
is dealing as many of these complaints and

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:42.479
issues at the first point of contact and training
our first tier of response so that they are

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:48.160
able to do that, and I think that has a big
impact, obviously. However, many of these

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.860
are more complex and take longer to deal with.
Malcolm Grant: Even with the complex ones,

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.709
I think that first point of personal contact
is a very good way of managing the process

00:08:57.709 --> 00:09:04.549
thereafter. Thank you very much. Are we content
to endorse the revised policy? Thank you very much.

